    <div class="profile-section-block" id="foundation">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Foundation</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/muhammad-bin-tughluq"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/muhammad-bin-tughluq.jpg" alt="Muhammad bin Tughluq" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/muhammad-bin-tughluq">Muhammad bin Tughluq</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Muhammad_bin_Tughlaq_crop.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Unknown author, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Bahmani Sultanate was born from a revolt against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/delhi-sultanate">Delhi Sultanate</a>. In the 1320s and 1330s <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muhammad-bin-tughluq">Muhammad bin Tughluq</a> had extended <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/delhi">Delhi</a>'s rule over the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/deccan">Deccan</a> and even moved much of the court and population of Delhi to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/daulatabad">Daulatabad</a>, the former <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seuna-yadava-dynasty">Yadava</a> capital of Devagiri, but his harsh fiscal policies and suspicion of provincial officers provoked repeated rebellions. In 1345–1347 the foreign-born military commanders of the Deccan, known as the <em>amiran-i sada</em>, rose against his governors and took Daulatabad. On 3 August 1347 one of their leaders, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muzaffar-shah-i">Zafar Khan</a>, also known as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ala-ud-din-bahman-shah">Hasan Gangu</a>, was proclaimed sultan as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alauddin-khalji">Alauddin</a> Hasan Bahman Shah. His dynastic name claimed descent from the legendary Persian king Bahman, although <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/firishta">Ferishta</a>'s later story that he had once served a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brahmins">Brahmin</a> named Gangu is probably a legend. He soon moved his capital to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kalaburagi">Gulbarga</a>, which he renamed Ahsanabad, in the heart of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/deccan-plateau">Deccan plateau</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tughlaq-dynasty">Tughluq</a> armies failed to recover the region, and by his death in 1358 Bahman Shah controlled a kingdom stretching from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabian-sea">Arabian Sea</a> coast to the borders of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/telangana">Telangana</a>, bounded in the south by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/krishna">Krishna</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tungabhadra-river">Tungabhadra</a> rivers, where it faced the newly founded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vijayanagara-empire">Vijayanagara Empire</a>.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="socioeconomy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Socioeconomy</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/bidar"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/bidar.jpg" alt="Bidar" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/bidar">Bidar</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Guru_Nanak_Jhira_Sahib_gurdwara_in_Bidar,_Jul_2017.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Sunnya343, CC BY-SA 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Bahmani economy rested on agriculture in the black <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cotton">cotton</a> soils and river valleys of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/deccan">Deccan</a>, producing <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/millet">millets</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rice">rice</a>, cotton and oilseeds. Revenue was collected largely through local <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindu</a> village and district officials who continued to manage the countryside much as before. Towns grew around the courts at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kalaburagi">Gulbarga</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bidar">Bidar</a> and around forts and markets, and textiles were a major product. The kingdom's western ports, such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chaul">Chaul</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dabhol">Dabhol</a>, and after 1472 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/goa">Goa</a>, connected it to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabian-sea">Arabian Sea</a> trade, importing horses from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabia">Arabia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persia">Persia</a>, which were essential for its cavalry, and exporting cloth and other goods. The Russian merchant <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/afanasy-nikitin">Afanasy Nikitin</a>, who stayed in Bidar around 1470, described the wealth of its nobles and the poverty of its peasants. Society was diverse. Most of the population was Hindu, while the ruling class included Deccani Muslims, descended from earlier settlers from northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, and newcomers from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iraq">Iraq</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-asia">Central Asia</a> and Arabia, known as <em><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/afaqis">Afaqis</a></em> or "westerners", as well as Abyssinians. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sufism">Sufi</a> orders were influential; the Chishti saint <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gesu-daraz">Sayyid Muhammad Gesu Daraz</a> settled at Gulbarga, where his shrine remains a major pilgrimage centre, and later sultans also patronised the descendants of the Persian Sufi <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shah-nimatullah-wali">Shah Nimatullah Wali</a>. Persian was the language of the court and administration.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="military">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Military</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/firuz-shah-bahmani"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/firuz-shah-bahmani.jpg" alt="Firuz Shah Bahmani" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/firuz-shah-bahmani">Firuz Shah Bahmani</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Haft_Gumbad.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Smloverboy4, CC BY-SA 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Bahmani army was built around cavalry, especially <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mounted-archer">mounted archers</a> on imported horses, commanded by nobles who held revenue assignments in return for maintaining troops. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/war-elephant">War elephants</a> and infantry levies supported them, and by the fifteenth century firearms were in use, although the precise date of their introduction is debated. Great fortresses anchored Bahmani power, among them <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kalaburagi">Gulbarga</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bidar">Bidar</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paranda">Parenda</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/golconda-fort">Golconda</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/raichur">Raichur</a>. For most of its history the sultanate fought the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vijayanagara-empire">Vijayanagara Empire</a> for control of the fertile <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/raichur-doab">Raichur Doab</a> between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/krishna">Krishna</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tungabhadra-river">Tungabhadra</a> rivers. The wars began under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muhammad-shah-of-brunei">Muhammad Shah</a> I (r. 1358–1375) and were marked, according to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/firishta">Ferishta</a>'s later chronicle, by great slaughter on both sides. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/firuz-shah-bahmani">Firuz Shah</a> (r. 1397–1422) won several campaigns, but in 1420 he suffered a heavy defeat at Pangal. In the east the Bahmanis fought the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/musunuri-nayakas">Musunuri Nayakas</a> and other Telugu chiefs; Muhammad Shah I received the fortress of Golconda and a celebrated turquoise throne from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kapaya-nayaka">Kapaya Nayaka</a>, and Ahmad Shah I (r. 1422–1436) overran <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/warangal">Warangal</a>. In 1461–1462 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahmud-khalji">Mahmud Khalji of Malwa</a> invaded and besieged Bidar but was driven back with help from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gujarat">Gujarat</a>. Under the minister <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahmud-gawan">Mahmud Gawan</a> the army conquered the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/konkan-coast">Konkan coast</a> and took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/goa">Goa</a> from Vijayanagara in 1472.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="governance">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Governance &amp; Law</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/delhi"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/delhi.jpg" alt="Delhi" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/delhi">Delhi</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Jama_Masjid_2011.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Marcin Białek, CC BY-SA 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The sultan stood at the head of a court modelled on Persian and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/delhi">Delhi</a> traditions, assisted by ministers such as the <em><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vakil">vakil</a>-us-sultanat</em>, or chief minister, and the <em>amir-i jumla</em>, in charge of finance. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muhammad-shah-of-brunei">Muhammad Shah</a> I organised the kingdom into four provinces, or <em>tarafs</em>, centred on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kalaburagi">Gulbarga</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/daulatabad">Daulatabad</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berar">Berar</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bidar">Bidar</a>, each under a governor, the <em>tarafdar</em>, who commanded the provincial army, collected revenue and appointed subordinates. This gave governors great power, and the rivalry between the Deccani and Afaqi nobility became the central political conflict of the court; in 1447 hundreds of Afaqi soldiers were massacred after a defeat at Chakan, and factional violence recurred. Ahmad Shah I moved the capital from Gulbarga to Bidar around 1429. The ablest administrator was <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahmud-gawan">Mahmud Gawan</a>, a Persian merchant from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gilan">Gilan</a> who served as chief minister under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muhammad-shah-iii-bahmani">Muhammad Shah III</a> (r. 1463–1482). He divided the four provinces into eight smaller ones, reserved some districts in each for the crown, surveyed land for revenue assessment and tightened control over military assignments, reforms that reduced the governors' independence. His enemies among the Deccani nobles forged a letter implicating him in treason, and in 1481 the sultan had him executed, a decision he is said to have regretted bitterly before his own death the following year.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="impact">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Global &amp; Regional Impact</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/jama-masjid-mandu"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/jama-masjid-mandu.jpg" alt="Jama Masjid, Mandu" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/jama-masjid-mandu">Jama Masjid, Mandu</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Inside_of_Jama_Masjid.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Intekhab0731, CC BY-SA 3.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Bahmani Sultanate established a lasting Islamic political culture in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/deccan">Deccan</a>, distinct from that of northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> and closely tied to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabian-sea">Arabian Sea</a> world. Its five successor states, the sultanates of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bijapur">Bijapur</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ahmadnagar">Ahmadnagar</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/golconda-fort">Golconda</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berar">Berar</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bidar">Bidar</a>, carried on its institutions, Persianate court culture and wars with Vijayanagara, and three of them survived until the Mughal conquests of the 1630s to 1680s. Bahmani architecture blended Persian, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/delhi">Delhi</a> and local Deccani elements. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jama-masjid-mandu">Jama Masjid</a> in the fort of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kalaburagi">Gulbarga</a>, built in 1367, is unusual for having its entire courtyard covered by domes, while the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madrasa">madrasa</a> built by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahmud-gawan">Mahmud Gawan</a> at Bidar in 1472, with its tile-faced minarets, and the painted royal tombs at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ashtur">Ashtur</a> reflect strong Iranian influence. The court patronised Persian poetry and history, and the Deccani form of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/urdu">Urdu</a>, which later flourished at Bijapur and Golconda, began to develop in this environment. The sultanate also left a long legacy of conflict: its wars with Vijayanagara shaped both states, and accounts of them, especially in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/firishta">Ferishta</a>'s history written around 1600, influenced later views of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindu</a>–Muslim relations in the south. The rivalry between Deccani and Afaqi elites continued in the successor kingdoms.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="decline">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Decline &amp; Fall</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/yusuf-adil-shah"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/yusuf-adil-shah.jpg" alt="Yusuf Adil Shah" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/yusuf-adil-shah">Yusuf Adil Shah</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:The_House_of_Bijapur_Album_leaf,_ca._1680_Deccan,_Bijapur._Metropolitan_Museum_of_Art.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Kamal Muhammad; Chand Muhammad, CC0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The execution of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahmud-gawan">Mahmud Gawan</a> in 1481 removed the minister who had held the kingdom together, and the death of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muhammad-shah-iii-bahmani">Muhammad Shah III</a> in 1482 left the throne to the young <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahmud-shah-bahmani">Mahmud Shah</a> (r. 1482–1518), who never gained real authority. Power at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bidar">Bidar</a> passed to the Turkic noble <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qasim-barid">Qasim Barid</a> and later to his son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amir-barid-i">Amir Barid</a>, while the provincial governors acted on their own. Around 1490 three of them effectively declared independence: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yusuf-adil-shah">Yusuf Adil Khan</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bijapur">Bijapur</a>, Ahmad Nizam ul-Mulk at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ahmadnagar">Ahmadnagar</a> and Fathullah Imad ul-Mulk in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berar">Berar</a>, founding the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/adil-shahi-dynasty">Adil Shahi</a>, Nizam Shahi and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/imad-shahi-dynasty">Imad Shahi</a> dynasties. Quli Qutb ul-Mulk, governor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/telangana">Telangana</a>, made himself independent at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/golconda-fort">Golconda</a> around 1518, though he formally assumed royal titles only later. The Barid family kept the remaining Bahmani sultans as puppets at Bidar, while <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/krishnadevaraya">Krishnadevaraya</a> of Vijayanagara exploited the disorder to take the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/raichur-doab">Raichur Doab</a> in 1520. After Mahmud Shah's death a series of short-lived sultans followed. The last, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kalimullah-shah">Kalimullah</a>, fled Bidar around 1527 and sought help from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/babur">Babur</a>, the new Mughal ruler of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/delhi">Delhi</a>, and later from Bijapur and Ahmadnagar, dying in exile. The Barid Shahis then ruled Bidar in their own name, and the Bahmani kingdom was divided among five <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/deccan-sultanates">Deccan sultanates</a>.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>The Bahmani Sultanate was the first independent Muslim state of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/deccan">Deccan</a>, whose Persianate court at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kalaburagi">Gulbarga</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bidar">Bidar</a>, administrative reforms under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahmud-gawan">Mahmud Gawan</a> and long wars with Vijayanagara shaped southern Indian politics and gave rise to the five <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/deccan-sultanates">Deccan sultanates</a> that succeeded it.</em></p>

